page ErrorE117(path: "/errors/E117", title: "E117 — A key combination no keyboard sends", description: "`on key(…)` names modifiers (`ctrl`, `shift`, `alt`, `meta`) and one key; a modifier it does not know, no key, or a key no keyboard has would never match.") {
    ErrorShell("E117", title: "A key combination no keyboard sends", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "`on key(…)` names modifiers (`ctrl`, `shift`, `alt`, `meta`) and one key; a modifier it does not know, no key, or a key no keyboard has would never match.", printed: #"error[E117]: `on key("ctrl+shfit+k")` names `shfit`, which is not a modifier
 --> src/App.wf:2:12
  |
2 |     on key("ctrl+shfit+k") { log("search") }
  |            ^
  = help: Modifiers `mod` (⌘ on a Mac, Ctrl elsewhere), `ctrl`, `shift`, `alt`, `meta` (`cmd`), then one key: a letter, a digit, or a name like `Enter`, `Escape`, `ArrowDown`, `Tab`, `F2`; a chord is its steps with a space between, `mod+k mod+s`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/E117"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E117.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    on key("ctrl+shfit+k") { log("search") }
    Heading("Search").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    on key("ctrl+shift+k") { log("search") }
    Heading("Search").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Modifiers `ctrl`, `shift`, `alt`, `meta` (or `cmd`), then one key:\na letter, a digit, or a name — `Enter`, `Escape`, `ArrowDown`, `Tab`, `F2`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorE118(path: "/errors/E118", title: "E118 — A name the language already has", description: "A name the language gives a meaning cannot be a program's own — not a `state`, `derived`, `persist` or `let`, a prop, a route or action parameter, a…") {
    ErrorShell("E118", title: "A name the language already has", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "A name the language gives a meaning cannot be a program's own — not a `state`, `derived`, `persist` or `let`, a prop, a route or action parameter, a loop item, a lambda's parameter, a binding of `if let`, `match` or a slot, nor a top-level `const`, `data`, `image`, store or `api`. They are `env`, the build's settings; `params`, the route's; `locale` and `dir`, the reader's language; the values the browser keeps current, `now`, `viewport`, `query`, `hash`, `theme`, `network` and `update`; the language's functions (`t`, `format`, `ago`, `log`, `navigate`, `fetch`, `uuid`, `sanitize`, `every`, `after`, …); the words a statement is written with (`state`, `action`, `let`, `if`, `show`, `match`, `style`, `on`, …); and, at the top level, the runtime's `WF`. Declared, one would hide what the language means by it for as far as its scope reaches. The names a handler brings (`event`, `e`, `value`, `key`) and the browser's globals are a program's to use.", printed: #"error[E118]: `env` is a name the language already has: the build's settings, read as `env.PUBLIC_API`
 --> src/App.wf:2:5
  |
2 |     state env = "staging"
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: Name it something of your own — `target` or `stage`: inside its scope every `env` would mean this, and the language's would have no name
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/E118"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: true, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E118.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"page Deploy(path: "/", title: "Deploy", description: "Pick a target.") {
    state env = "staging"
    Heading("Deploy to {env}").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"page Deploy(path: "/", title: "Deploy", description: "Pick a target.") {
    state target = "staging"
    Heading("Deploy to {target}").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Name it something of your own — `state target = \"staging\"` — and\nits reads with it. The message suggests one where there is a usual choice:\n`target` or `stage` for `env`, `search` for `query`, `scheme` for `theme`,\n`item` or a word for what it holds for `t`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
        Markdown("A name the language gives a meaning is not a program's to declare — not as\na `state`, `derived`, `persist` or `let`, a prop, a route or action\nparameter, a loop item, a lambda's parameter, a binding of `if let`, `match`\nor a slot, nor a top-level `const`, `data`, `image`, store or `api`.\nDeclared, it would hide what the language means by it for as far as its\nscope reaches: a `state env` hid the build's settings in its page, and a\nloop's `t` hid the translation function inside the loop. The names are:\n\n- `env`, the build's settings; `params`, the route's; `locale` and `dir`,\n  the reader's language;\n- the values the browser keeps current: `now`, `viewport`, `query`, `hash`,\n  `theme`, `network`, `update`;\n- the language's functions: `t`, `format`, `ago`, `log`, `navigate`,\n  `fetch`, `uuid`, `sanitize`, `setLocale`, `setTheme`, `optimistic`,\n  `beacon`, `animate`, `replayAnimation`, `every`, `after`, `ws`, `sse`,\n  `broadcast`, `rtc`;\n- the words a statement is written with: `state`, `persist`, `derived`,\n  `effect`, `action`, `use`, `resource`, `validate`, `socket`, `stream`,\n  `channel`, `peer`, `head`, `slot`, `part`, `if`, `else`, `for`, `in`, `by`,\n  `show`, `match`, `children`, `emit`, `sequence`, `on`, `style`,\n  `transition`, `let`, `return`, `await`, `try`, `catch`;\n- at the top level, `WF`, the runtime the compiled pages call;\n- in a store, the names every JavaScript object has — `valueOf`,\n  `toString`, `constructor`, `hasOwnProperty`, `isPrototypeOf`,\n  `propertyIsEnumerable`, `toLocaleString`, `__proto__` and its\n  `__define…`/`__lookup…` kin: a store is an object whose members are its\n  own, and a `valueOf` member replaced the one JavaScript calls itself, so\n  the page threw as soon as it was drawn;\n- and, as a prop, `__proto__`, which in the object a call passes sets its\n  prototype instead (a prop named `toString` or `valueOf` is fine).\n\nThe names a handler brings with it — `event`, `e`, `value`, `key` — and the\nbrowser's globals (`window`, `history`, …) are a program's to use, and so is\nany name as a field, a map key or an enum case: `type Box \{ env: String \}`\nand `.env` are fine.")
    }
}

page ErrorE901(path: "/errors/E901", title: "E901 — The compiler wrote JavaScript a browser would refuse", description: "The build reads back every script it writes, and this one would not run. It is a bug in WebFluent, not in the program; please report it with the…") {
    ErrorShell("E901", title: "The compiler wrote JavaScript a browser would refuse", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "The build reads back every script it writes, and this one would not run. It is a bug in WebFluent, not in the program; please report it with the source that produced it.", printed: #""#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: true, example: false, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E901.md") {
        fixed {
            Stack(gap: .sm) {
                Markdown("please report it, with\nthe source that produced it.")
            }
        }
        Markdown("The build reads back every script it writes; this one would not run. It is\na bug in WebFluent, not in your program.")
    }
}

page ErrorE902(path: "/errors/E902", title: "E902 — Output its own security policy refuses", description: "The build holds every page to the content security policy it ships beside it — as it wrote the page, and as the page's own `head \{ \}` tags would add…") {
    ErrorShell("E902", title: "Output its own security policy refuses", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "The build holds every page to the content security policy it ships beside it — as it wrote the page, and as the page's own `head \{ \}` tags would add to it; something there would be blocked.", printed: #""#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: true, example: false, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E902.md") {
        fixed {
            Stack(gap: .sm) {
                Markdown("what the\nmessage says; a library's origin goes in `meta.scripts`, a stylesheet's in\n`meta.stylesheets`.")
            }
        }
        Markdown("The build holds every page it writes to the content security policy it\nships beside it; something on this page — an inline script, a `style=`, a\nscript from an origin the policy never named — would be blocked. A page's\nown `head \{ script(src: …) \}` or `head \{ link(rel: \"stylesheet\", href: …) \}`\nis held to it too, where it is written: the tag is added on the live page,\nand the browser would refuse it there without a word.")
    }
}

page ErrorE119(path: "/errors/E119", title: "E119 — A hidden direction control in the source", description: "An embedding, override or isolate control (U+202A–U+202E, U+2066–U+2069) is invisible and reorders what follows it, so the code a reader sees is not…") {
    ErrorShell("E119", title: "A hidden direction control in the source", severity: "warning", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "An embedding, override or isolate control (U+202A–U+202E, U+2066–U+2069) is invisible and reorders what follows it, so the code a reader sees is not the code that runs (Trojan Source).", printed: #""#, quickfix: "", lowerable: true, why: true, example: false, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E119.md") {
        fixed {
            Stack(gap: .sm) {
                Markdown("remove it. Arabic beside Latin needs no control: each run\ntakes the direction of its own letters.\n\nNames may be written in Arabic, or any script: a name starts with a letter\nand goes on with letters, digits, `_` and the marks of its letters, as\nUAX #31 says (`state عددٌ = 3`), and is one name however its letters were\ntyped — أ precomposed or ا with a combining hamza (NFC).")
            }
        }
        Markdown("Warning. An embedding, override or isolate control — U+202A to U+202E,\nU+2066 to U+2069 — is invisible, and it reorders what follows it: a\ncomment that seems to close, or a string that seems to hold one word, can\nhide code that runs (Trojan Source, CVE-2021-42574). The editor draws each\nas a mark; the check names each by its line, its column and its Unicode\nname.")
    }
}

page ErrorE120(path: "/errors/E120", title: "E120 — Something nests too deeply", description: "Brackets, blocks, components that place each other, or the links of one chain (`a.b.c`, `1 + 2 + 3`) nest more than 100 levels deep, which no pass of…") {
    ErrorShell("E120", title: "Something nests too deeply", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "Brackets, blocks, components that place each other, or the links of one chain (`a.b.c`, `1 + 2 + 3`) nest more than 100 levels deep, which no pass of the compiler follows.", printed: #""#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: true, example: false, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E120.md") {
        fixed {
            Stack(gap: .sm) {
                Markdown("name a part of it — a `let` in an action, a `derived` value, a\ncomponent — and use the name. A component that places itself stops when\nits data does; one whose data does not end is this error too.")
            }
        }
        Markdown("Error. Brackets inside brackets, blocks inside blocks, a splice inside a\nstring inside a splice, the links of one chain (`a.b.c`, `1 + 2 + 3`,\neach link nesting the value one level deeper) and, in a template,\ncomponents that place one another — counted together, more than 100\nlevels deep. The parser, the checker and the template engine each stop\nthere and say so, where each would otherwise run out of stack. The\ndeepest program of this guide, the examples and the site nests 15 levels.")
    }
}

page ErrorE121(path: "/errors/E121", title: "E121 — A render that would do too much", description: "A template's render would draw more elements and loop items, work out more, or write more markup than one render may: nested loops over vast ranges…") {
    ErrorShell("E121", title: "A render that would do too much", severity: "error", family: "Syntax and structure", summary: "A template's render would draw more elements and loop items, work out more, or write more markup than one render may: nested loops over vast ranges or data, or an expression that makes more than one evaluation may.", printed: #""#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: true, example: false, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix")), Anchor(id: "why", label: t("error.why"))], source: "md-docs/errors/E121.md") {
        fixed {
            Stack(gap: .sm) {
                Markdown("draw less — page the data before it reaches the\ntemplate, or work the value out on the server and hand it over.")
            }
        }
        Markdown("Error, from the template engine (`wf render`, `Template::render_*`). One\nrender may take ten million steps — an element drawn, a loop item, a unit\nof working a value out — and write 256 MB of markup, and one expression\nmay make about half a million list items or 32 MB of text. A render past\nany of them stops and says where, instead of growing until the server\nruns out of memory: two nested `for` loops over ranges of a billion, a\nloop over the data inside a loop over the data that is too large, or\n`(1..=1000000).map(i => 1..=1000000)`. A build that meets such an\nexpression at build time leaves it to the page, as it does any value it\ncannot work out.")
    }
}

page ErrorT01(path: "/errors/T01", title: "T01 — A value of the wrong type", description: "A value given to a prop, a state, a field, a parameter or an assignment that is not of the type it is declared to hold.") {
    ErrorShell("T01", title: "A value of the wrong type", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A value given to a prop, a state, a field, a parameter or an assignment that is not of the type it is declared to hold.", printed: #"error[T01]: `count` is `String`, but `Number` is wanted
 --> src/App.wf:4:34
  |
4 |     Button("Reset") { on click { count = "zero" } }
  |                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: Convert it: `Number(value)`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T01"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T01.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state count: Number = 0
    Heading("Counter").h1
    Button("Reset") { on click { count = "zero" } }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state count: Number = 0
    Heading("Count {count}").h1
    Button("Reset") { on click { count = 0 } }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Give the state a value of its type, or convert: `Number(text)`, `\"\{n\}\"`. If the state really holds either, declare it `Any`.\n\nA number a built-in cannot be given is one too: a `Carousel(interval:)`\noutside 1 to 2147483647 milliseconds (`-5000` used to become a 0 ms\nautoplay), a `Column(span:)` that is not a whole number from 1 to 12.\n\n```wf expect=T01\npage P(path: \"/\", title: \"T\", description: \"D\") \{\n    Heading(\"Grid\").h1\n    Row \{ Column(span: 13) \{ Text(\"Wide\") \} \}\n\}\n```\n\n```text\nerror[T01]: `span` of Column is 13, but a whole number of the 12 columns, from 1 to 12, is wanted\n --> src/App.wf:3:11\n  |\n3 |     Row \{ Column(span: 13) \{ Text(\"Wide\") \} \}\n  |           ^^^^^^\n  = help: `span: 6` is half the row\n  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T01\n```")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT02(path: "/errors/T02", title: "T02 — A case the enum does not have", description: "`.loud` given where an enum is wanted that has no case `loud`.") {
    ErrorShell("T02", title: "A case the enum does not have", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "`.loud` given where an enum is wanted that has no case `loud`.", printed: #"error[T02]: `Tone` has no case `.quiet`
 --> src/App.wf:3:17
  |
3 |     state tone: Tone = .quiet
  |                 ^^^^
  = help: `Tone` takes .calm, .loud
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T02"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T02.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"enum Tone { calm, loud }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state tone: Tone = .quiet
    Heading("Tone").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"enum Tone { calm, loud }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state tone: Tone = .calm
    Heading("Tone: {tone}").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Use one of the cases the message lists, or add the case to the `enum`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT04(path: "/errors/T04", title: "T04 — A value that may be null, read as if it were not", description: "A field or method read from a value that may be `null`. `if let`, `??`, `?.` or a `!= null` check narrows it.") {
    ErrorShell("T04", title: "A value that may be null, read as if it were not", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A field or method read from a value that may be `null`. `if let`, `??`, `?.` or a `!= null` check narrows it.", printed: #"error[T04]: `user.email` may be null, so `.length` may fail
 --> src/App.wf:5:12
  |
5 |     Text("{user.email.length} characters")
  |            ^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: Unwrap it first: `if let x = value { … }`, `value ?? fallback`, `value?.field`, or a check for `!= null`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T04"#, quickfix: "Read it through null: `?.length`", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T04.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"type Member { name: String, email: String? }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state user = Member(name: "Ada", email: null)
    Heading("Profile").h1
    Text("{user.email.length} characters")
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"type Member { name: String, email: String? }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state user = Member(name: "Ada", email: null)
    Heading("Profile").h1
    Text("{user.email?.length ?? 0} characters")
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Unwrap it: `if let e = user.email \{ e.length \}`, `user.email?.length ?? 0`, or `(user.email ?? \"\").length`. Your editor's quick fix, and `wf fix --code T04`, write the `?.` for you.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT05(path: "/errors/T05", title: "T05 — A field or method that does not exist", description: "A record, a map literal or a built-in value read for a field or method it does not have.") {
    ErrorShell("T05", title: "A field or method that does not exist", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A record, a map literal or a built-in value read for a field or method it does not have.", printed: #"error[T05]: `User` has no field `nmae`
 --> src/App.wf:4:18
  |
4 |     Heading(user.nmae).h1
  |                  ^^^^
  = help: Did you mean `name`? Its fields are `name`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T05"#, quickfix: "Change to `name`", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T05.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"type User { name: String }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state user = User(name: "Ada")
    Heading(user.nmae).h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"type User { name: String }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state user = User(name: "Ada")
    Heading(user.name).h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Correct the spelling to one of the fields listed, or add the field to the `type`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT06(path: "/errors/T06", title: "T06 — A member a store or service does not have", description: "A store's state, derived value or action, or a service's endpoint, that it does not declare.") {
    ErrorShell("T06", title: "A member a store or service does not have", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A store's state, derived value or action, or a service's endpoint, that it does not declare.", printed: #"error[T06]: `Cart` has no member `count`
 --> src/App.wf:4:20
  |
4 |     Heading("{Cart.count} items").h1
  |                    ^^^^^
  = help: Its members are `items`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T06"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T06.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"store Cart { state items = [] }
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    use Cart
    Heading("{Cart.count} items").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"store Cart {
    state items = []
    derived count = items.length
}
page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    use Cart
    Heading("{Cart.count} items").h1
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Use a member the store declares, or declare it: `derived count = items.length`. For a service, the endpoint must be declared in its `api`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT07(path: "/errors/T07", title: "T07 — A condition that is always true", description: "A list, a record or an action used as a condition: it is never `false`, so the branch always runs.") {
    ErrorShell("T07", title: "A condition that is always true", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A list, a record or an action used as a condition: it is never `false`, so the branch always runs.", printed: #"error[T07]: `if` reads `items` as a condition, but a list is always true
 --> src/App.wf:4:5
  |
4 |     if items { Text("There are items") }
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: Ask about its length: `items.length > 0`
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T07"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T07.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state items = ["a"]
    Heading("List").h1
    if items { Text("There are items") }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state items = ["a"]
    Heading("List").h1
    if items.length > 0 { Text("There are items") }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Ask the question you mean: `items.length > 0`, `user != null`.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT08(path: "/errors/T08", title: "T08 — A loop over something that is not a list", description: "A `for` over a number, a string or a record.") {
    ErrorShell("T08", title: "A loop over something that is not a list", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "A `for` over a number, a string or a record.", printed: #"error[T08]: `for` loops over a list, but `total` is `Number`
 --> src/App.wf:4:5
  |
4 |     for n in total { Text("{n}") }
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = help: Give it a list, or `.split(…)` a string first
  = docs: https://webfluent.monzeromer.dev/errors/T08"#, quickfix: "", lowerable: false, why: false, example: true, families: ERROR_INDEX, related: [], sections: [Anchor(id: "printed", label: t("error.printed")), Anchor(id: "fix", label: t("error.fix"))], source: "md-docs/errors/T08.md") {
        broken { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state total = 3
    Heading("Loop").h1
    for n in total { Text("{n}") }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        fixed { CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "T", description: "D") {
    state total = 3
    Heading("Loop").h1
    for n in 1..=total { Text("{n}") }
}"#, title: "wf") }
        note {
            Markdown("Loop over a list — `for n in 1..=total` for a range of numbers.")
            Markdown("Each example is a whole program: it is all of `src/App.wf` in a project with no other `.wf` file ([trying an example](/errors#trying-an-example)).") { style { font-size: 14px; color: $muted } }
        }
    }
}

page ErrorT09(path: "/errors/T09", title: "T09 — An emit that does not match its event", description: "The arguments an `emit` passes are not the ones the event declares.") {
    ErrorShell("T09", title: "An emit that does not match its event", severity: "error", family: "Types", summary: "The arguments an `emit` passes are not the ones the event declares.", printed: #"error[T09]: `emit change` passes 2 arguments, but the event takes 1
